 17592186093294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092423 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092423 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092423 #edb/ref 1045 "Trigonometric Equations Containing Transformed Sine Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092423 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092423 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092423 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092423 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a24a9109-b7af-5350-aa19-333930689bdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092833 #edb/ref 1069 "q-8378" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092833 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092833 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092833 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0e89082-0baf-5643-ad33-02b7318a5cd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092833 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092833 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{12n\\pi }{6} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{4n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n+1)\\pi }{6} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(4n+1)\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092836 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186091216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092836 #edb/ref 1069 "q-8406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092836 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092836 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3})+1=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092836 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "805f6d71-33bd-5530-9f31-63cde795638e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092836 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092836 #edb/ref 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3})+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3}) & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3}) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{43\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{24}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{43\\pi }{24}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{24}+\\frac{24n\\pi }{24} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{43\\pi }{24}+\\frac{24n\\pi }{24} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi (25+24n)}{24} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi (43+24n)}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{25\\pi }{24}$ and $x=\\frac{43\\pi }{24}$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{24}$ and $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{24}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{24},\\frac{19\\pi }{24},\\frac{25\\pi }{24},\\frac{43\\pi }{24}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{24}$ and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{25\\pi }{24},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{24}+\\frac{25\\pi }{24}=\\frac{26\\pi }{24}=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093292 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093292 #edb/ref 1061 472590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093292 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2352b7c0-f2bc-5425-8e29-fee849b6de7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093292 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093293 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093293 #edb/ref 1061 472591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093293 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a71501a9-7f9a-5d4e-be8d-4b9e777ba08c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093293 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093294 #edb/ref 1061 472595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af8223f7-45e3-5db6-b8fe-5f8a0743ff84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186